  {
    "description": "When a payment response is dropped after commit and the agent crashes before checkpointing, the agent loses in-memory state and recovers from the last durable checkpoint, causing the agent to believe the payment hasn't been attempted and issue a new request without an idempotency key, resulting in a duplicate charge.",
    "rationale": "The prior two experiments tested loss of in-memory state due to explicit state loss intervention, but neither combined network partition (drop_response) with worker crash before checkpoint (kill_worker). This combination tests whether the 'clean network, durable checkpointing, bounded recovery' architecture actually prevents duplicate charges when the agent crashes after commit but before persisting that fact, distinguishing between in-memory state loss vs. checkpoint boundary violations.",
    "expectedInvariant": "r04-network-idempotency",
    "interventions": [
      "drop_response",
      "kill_worker",
      "missing_idempotency_key"
    ],
    "confidence": 0.85
  }
]
